# ============================================================================
# ATTACK on exp1 / exp6 - the complement operators are unsound for any probe
# that is not time-constant. One root cause, stated and then repaired.
# ============================================================================
# A table is written { A } | { B }. A tau constant is read under an implicit
# `always`, so that value is (always A) OR (always B) - NOT always(A OR B).
# The two differ, and the gap is exactly the set of streams that move
# between rows over time. Every operator built on the COMPLEMENT of a table
# (exp1's MEMBERSHIP and DIFFERENCE, exp6's ADDED and REMOVED) inherits the
# gap: a row that sits in the table at every single instant is nevertheless
# outside it as a value, so DIFFERENCE keeps it and the audit diff flags it.
#
# Columns are sbf here on purpose: the same attack in bv[8] is real but the
# engine prints cvc5 translation failures alongside the verdict
# (see opus_cvc5_faildopen.md), and an attack should not need a caveat.
# Keys are two bits: key2 = (hi=1,lo=0), key3 = (hi=1,lo=1).
#   PRICED = { hi=1,lo=0 } | { hi=1,lo=1 }        the priced-key table
#   P      = { [t<3] -> key2 } && { [t>=3] -> key3 }   one time-varying row
# Run: tau -q < opus_attack_exp1_difference.tau
# EXPECTED-RESULTS: T T T T T T T F

set charvar off
set maxsplits 1

# (1) PRICED is a live table:
n ({ okhi[t]=1 && oklo[t]=0 } | { okhi[t]=1 && oklo[t]=1 }) != 0
# Answer: T

# (2) P is a live row:
n { ([t < 3] -> (okhi[t]=1 && oklo[t]=0)) && ([t >= 3] -> (okhi[t]=1 && oklo[t]=1)) } != 0
# Answer: T

# (3) P's key is a PRICED key at EVERY instant: P entails always(okhi=1),
# and okhi=1 holds in both priced rows and in neither unpriced one.
n ({ ([t < 3] -> (okhi[t]=1 && oklo[t]=0)) && ([t >= 3] -> (okhi[t]=1 && oklo[t]=1)) } & { okhi[t]=1 }') = 0
# Answer: T

# (4) THE ATTACK: P nevertheless SURVIVES DIFFERENCE against PRICED - the
# operator reports a row as unpriced whose key is priced at every step.
n ({ ([t < 3] -> (okhi[t]=1 && oklo[t]=0)) && ([t >= 3] -> (okhi[t]=1 && oklo[t]=1)) } & ({ okhi[t]=1 && oklo[t]=0 } | { okhi[t]=1 && oklo[t]=1 })') != 0
# Answer: T - (3) and (4) together are the contradiction.

# (5) CONTROL - a time-CONSTANT priced row is correctly swallowed, which is
# why the bug is invisible to every test written with constant rows:
n ({ okhi[t]=1 && oklo[t]=0 } & ({ okhi[t]=1 && oklo[t]=0 } | { okhi[t]=1 && oklo[t]=1 })') = 0
# Answer: T

# (6) THE ROOT CAUSE, one direction: the join of alwayses entails the
# always of the join.
n (({ okhi[t]=1 && oklo[t]=0 } | { okhi[t]=1 && oklo[t]=1 }) & { (okhi[t]=1 && oklo[t]=0) || (okhi[t]=1 && oklo[t]=1) }') = 0
# Answer: T

# (7) THE ROOT CAUSE, strictly: the converse fails - always-of-join has
# models that the join-of-alwayses does not. That gap is the attack.
n ({ (okhi[t]=1 && oklo[t]=0) || (okhi[t]=1 && oklo[t]=1) } & ({ okhi[t]=1 && oklo[t]=0 } | { okhi[t]=1 && oklo[t]=1 })') != 0
# Answer: T

# (8) THE REPAIR: build the table as ONE constant with an internal
# disjunction. P is then correctly a member, and DIFFERENCE drops it.
n ({ ([t < 3] -> (okhi[t]=1 && oklo[t]=0)) && ([t >= 3] -> (okhi[t]=1 && oklo[t]=1)) } & { (okhi[t]=1 && oklo[t]=0) || (okhi[t]=1 && oklo[t]=1) }') != 0
# Answer: F - the meet with the complement is empty: P is inside the
# repaired table, so the repaired DIFFERENCE correctly keeps nothing.
